When it was as if it were said: "Why does He give them respite and not deal with them by seizing them before inflicting harm upon His allies, the people of His friendship and His chosen ones?" He said: "That" (*dhalika*), i.e., the seizing in this condition, ﴿is because Allah﴾, i.e., because of the fact that they changed what was in themselves. It was within His power, may He be glorified, to seize them before they changed, given His knowledge of what is in their hearts. However, He, exalted is He, carried out His divine *sunnah* (way or established practice) so that His knowledge would be complete, His power perfect, and His encompassing of all the attributes of perfection fully realized, in that: ﴿He would not be one who⦆ (*lam yak*). This is how the original would have read, but it was omitted for brevity, to bring the statement closer and to make the hearer think in terms of its general cause. The *nun* of *yakun* was dropped as guidance that this admonition deserves to be delivered in the most concise form, so that it is hastened to be conveyed, because of the great benefit in receiving it well; for whoever opposes it is deserving of swift retribution. ﴿Changing a blessing⦆, i.e., whether small or great. He made clear that there is no blessing upon anyone except from Him, so He said: ﴿that He had bestowed upon a people⦆, i.e., whatever group they were, ﴿until they change⦆, i.e., they alter ﴿what⦆ they believe,

﴿in themselves⦆, with something other than it, that which is innate in them and hidden from them, while they assume their attribution of its opposite, which is manifest to them as an innate attribution. ﴿And that⦆, i.e., and because ﴿Allah⦆, He to whom belongs all perfection, ﴿is All-Hearing⦆, i.e., of what they deny to the messengers and of their statements; indeed, what they display is their true description,

﴿All-Knowing⦆, i.e., of what their hearts conceal of other than that, even if they themselves are unaware of it. So He tests them with a trial that brings forth that hidden thing and reveals every guarded secret. When knowledge of it becomes manifest, the judgment is enforced with overpowering force, for the complete establishment of proof. And because of His complete knowledge of their condition, He grants them respite. One only hastens when he fears that his insight may fail or that his knowledge may change. As for the One whose knowledge of outward appearances and inner hearts is equal, both states are the same to Him. So He grants respite for the completion of wisdom, and He does not neglect whoever deserves vengeance. That change which the trial made manifest is the denial of the truth out of obstinacy, and the turning away from the justice and the adherence to the paths of reason that they claimed, and the shunning of shamelessness in obstinacy, and the purity from the ways of corruption. Thus was every nation to which messengers were sent: they claimed one thing, while the opposite of that was concealed within their hearts and hidden in their inner selves, which they did not know, just as you see of most people you live with: a person thinks of himself what is not in him, and when tested, the reality contradicts him. When the messengers came to them and made the matter clear to them with a clarity admitting no confusion, they denied them, and so they changed what had been in their souls of what they had been claiming;
